<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.12.2: Equations and Their Graphs</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01462</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.12.2: Equations and Their Graphs</md:title>
    <md:uuid>4403350e-aa93-4a7e-966c-88fdfc67de74</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="practice" id="sect-00001">      <title>Activity</title>
      <note id="note-00001">
        <para id="para-00001">Access the <link url="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/b62c9150250bf52d6cc37569213ab516e0090dca">Desmos guide PDF</link> for tips on solving problems with the Desmos graphing calculator.</para></note>
      
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00001">
        <item>Each of the following functions, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>j</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, represents the amount of money in a bank account, in dollars, as a function of time <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, in years. They are each written in the form <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>a</m:mi><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:msup><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>.
 </item>
      </list>
      <list id="list-00002">
        <item><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>50</m:mn><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:msup><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></item>
        <item><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>50</m:mn><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:msup><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></item>
        <item><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>50</m:mn><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></item>
        <item><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>j</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>50</m:mn><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0.5</m:mn><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></item>
      </list>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00003">
        <item>Use graphing technology to graph each function on the same coordinate plane. It may be helpful to use a different color for each function.</item>
      </list><media alt="">
      <iframe src="../../media/desmos/c2b2be1392eb25bd3ede57495bbc1721230ec338.html" width="500" height="400"/></media>
        <exercise id="exer-00001">
          <problem id="prob-00001">
            <para id="para-00002">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00001">
            <para id="para-00003">Compare your answers:</para>
            <para id="para-00004"> <media alt=""><image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_14_03_01.jpg"/></media></para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00005">Explain how changing the value of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math><emphasis> </emphasis>changes the graph.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00006">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00007">When <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is greater than 1, larger values of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> mean that the function grows more quickly as <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> increases. A positive value of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> that is less than 1 means the function is decreasing as <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> increases.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00004">
        <item>Here are equations defining functions <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>p</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>q</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>r</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>. They are also written in the form <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>a</m:mi><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:msup><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>.</item>
      </list>
      <list id="list-00005">
        <item><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>p</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>10</m:mn><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:msup><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></item>
        <item><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>q</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>40</m:mn><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:msup><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></item>
        <item><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>r</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>100</m:mn><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:msup><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></item>
      </list>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00006">
        <item>Use graphing technology to graph each function on the same coordinate plane. It may be helpful to use a different color for each function.</item>
      </list><media alt="">
      <iframe src="../../media/desmos/58f9ed2fb53f011525001aa87dd0aa7dca68a98e.html" width="500" height="400"/></media>
        <exercise id="exer-00003">
          <problem id="prob-00003">
            <para id="para-00008">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00003">
            <para id="para-00009">Compare your answers:</para>
            <para id="para-00010"> <media alt=""><image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_14_03_02.jpg"/></media></para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      <exercise id="exer-00004">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00011">Explain how changing the value of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>a</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> changes the graph.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00012">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00013">It changes the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept to <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mi>a</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00007">
        <item>Here are equations defining functions <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> written in the form <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>a</m:mi><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:msup><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mi>d</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>.</item>
      </list>
      <list id="list-00008">
        <item><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></item>
        <item><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></item>
        <item><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></item>
      </list>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00009">
        <item>Use graphing technology to graph each function on the same coordinate plane. It may be helpful to use a different color for each function.</item>
      </list><media alt="">
      <iframe src="../../media/desmos/18ca89438df0b4a8759f9c4ca7ec43a014595aa4.html" width="500" height="400"/></media>
        <exercise id="exer-00005">
          <problem id="prob-00005">
            <para id="para-00014">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00005">
            <para id="para-00015">Compare your answers:</para>
            <para id="para-00016"> <media alt=""><image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_14_03_03.jpg"/></media></para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00010">
        <item>Explain how changing the value of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>a</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> changes the graph.</item>
      </list>
      
        <exercise id="exer-00006">
          <problem id="prob-00006">
            <para id="para-00017">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00006">
            <para id="para-00018">Compare your answers:</para>
                  <media alt="GRAPH OF THREE EXPONENTIAL GROWTH FUNCTIONS, LABELED F, G, AND H. FUNCTION G HAS A \(y\)-intercepts OF 1. FUNCTION F HAS A \(y\)-intercepts OF 3. FUNCTION H HAS A \(y\)-intercepts OF NEGATIVE 1.">
                <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_14_03_04.jpg"/>
              </media>
            
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      <exercise id="exer-00007">
        <problem id="prob-00007">
          <para id="para-00019">Discuss with a partner how changing the value of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>d</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> changes the asymptote of the graph.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00007">
          <para id="para-00020">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00021">Changing the value of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>d</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> changes the horizontal asymptote from <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> to <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>d</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>. So function <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> has a horizontal asymptote of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>, and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> has a horizontal asymptote of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise></section>
      <!-- "Start "Are you Ready for More? click to reveal.  If you have more than one on a page, you will need to change the Data-fire/data-wait-for-events for each set.-->
      
      <section id="sect-00002">
        <title>Are you ready for more?</title>
          <section id="sect-00003">
            <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
      
      <!--Start interaction.  If multiple interactions appear under "are you ready for more?, they should all have matching "data-wait-for-event", which should also match the "data-fire-event" for the button. Note in this sample, they are all RFM1-->
      <exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00008">
        <problem id="prob-00008">
          <para id="para-00022">As before, consider bank accounts whose balances are given by the following functions:</para>
          <para id="para-00023"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>10</m:mn><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:msup><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00027"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00028"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:msup><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00024">Which function would you choose? Does your choice depend on <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00008">
          <para id="para-00025">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00026">We can rewrite each of the functions as multiples of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:msup><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math> to more easily compare them:</para>
          <para id="para-00029"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>10</m:mn><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:msup><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00042"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:msup><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:msup><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:msup><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>9</m:mn><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:msup><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00043"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:msup><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:msup><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:msup><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>27</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:msup><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>13.5</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:msup><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00030">Since <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>9</m:mn><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mn>10</m:mn><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mn>13.5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>, we see that <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> has the most money for any value of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--End interaction-->
    </section>
  </section>
    <section id="sect-00004">
      <title>Video: Exponential Equations and How They Affect the Graphs</title>
      <para id="para-00031">Watch the following video to learn more about exponential functions and how they affect the graphs:</para>
      <media alt="Placeholder for Alt Text">
        <iframe width="660" height="371.4" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/c418a58864a702dfb9a80d1ba26aeeb7ce0b4724"/>
      </media>
    </section>
    <note class="self-check" id="note-00002">
      <exercise id="exer-00010">
        <problem id="prob-00010">
        <para id="para-00032"><link class="os-embed" url="#exercise/Alg1_5_12_2_SC"/></para>
        </problem>
        </exercise>
        </note>

        <section id="sect-00005">
          <title>Additional Resources</title>
          
    <section id="sect-00006">
      <title>Vertical Shifts with Exponential Functions</title>
      <para id="para-00033">The first transformation occurs when we add a constant <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>d</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> to the parent function <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>, giving us a vertical shift <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>d</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> units in the same direction as the sign. For example, if we begin by graphing a parent function, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>, we can then graph two vertical shifts alongside it, using <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>d</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>: the upward shift, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>, and the downward shift, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>. Both vertical shifts are shown in the graph below.</para>
      <para id="para-00034">
          <media alt="GRAPH OF THREE EXPONENTIAL GROWTH FUNCTIONS. F OF X HAS A \(y\)-intercepts OF 1 AND HORIZONTAL ASYMPTOTE OF Y EQUALS 0. G OF X HAS A \(y\)-intercepts OF 4 AND HORIZONTAL ASYMPTOTE OF Y EQUALS 3. H OF X HAS A \(y\)-intercepts OF NEGATIVE 2 AND HORIZONTAL ASYMPTOTE OF Y EQUALS NEGATIVE 3.">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_14_05_01.jpg" width="300"/>
          </media>
        
    </para>
      <para id="para-00035">Observe the results of shifting <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math> vertically:</para>
      <list id="list-00011">
        <item>The domain, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mo>∞</m:mo><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mo>∞</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>, remains unchanged.</item>
        <item>When the function is shifted up 3 units to <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>:   <list id="list-00012">
          <item>The <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept shifts up 3 units to <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>.</item>
          <item>The asymptote shifts up 3 units to <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</item>
          <item>The range becomes <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mo>∞</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>.</item>
        </list></item>
     
        <item>When the function is shifted down 3 units to <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>:    <list id="list-00013">
          <item>The <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept shifts down 3 units to <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>.</item>
          <item>The asymptote also shifts down 3 units to <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</item>
          <item>The range becomes <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mo>∞</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>.</item>
        </list></item>
    
      </list>
      <note class="try" id="note-00003">
  <title>Try It: Vertical Shifts with Exponential Functions</title>
      
        <exercise id="exer-00009">
          <problem id="prob-00009">
            <para id="para-00036">Where would the horizontal asymptote be located for the graph of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:msup><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>?</para>
            <para id="para-00037">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00009">
            <para id="para-00038">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00039">Here is how to find the equation of the horizontal asymptote:</para>
            <para id="para-00040">In the function <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:msup><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>, the vertical shift is represented by –6.</para>
            <para id="para-00041">The parent function, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>, has a horizontal asymptote at <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>. For the function <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:msup><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>, the horizontal asymptote will be shifted down 6 to <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </note>
    </section>
        </section>
         
  </content>
</document>